<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiburcio Carías Andino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tiburcio Carias Andino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiburcio Carías Andino (1876–1969) was a Honduran general and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> who was president from 1933 to 1949. He extended his term through constitutional changes, ruled as a dictator with the backing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> fruit companies and suppressed opposition. His long rule, known as the Cariato, brought order but little reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiburcio-carias-andino.jpg" alt="Tiburcio Carías Andino" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing, photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pres._Andino_of_Honduras_LCCN2016878325.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tiburcio Carías Andino, born in 1876 to Calixto Carías Galindo and Sara Francisca Andino Rivera, studied criminal law at the Central University of Honduras. In 1907 he fought alongside liberal forces and was promoted to general after the Battle of Lizapa; President Miguel R. Dávila Cuéllar then made him governor of the Copán Department. He became prominent in the civil wars of 1919 and 1924 and in March and April 1924 served briefly as chief of the revolution. He lost the 1928 election to Vicente Mejía Colindres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> and accepted the result in a rare peaceful transfer of power between the two main parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Carías won the 1932 election by about 20,000 votes, as Mejía Colindres resisted pressure from his own party to manipulate the result. His rule became the longest by one individual in Honduran history. He crushed a Liberal revolt, improved the military, gained the support of the banana companies by opposing strikes, and kept up payments on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</p><p>Because the constitution barred immediate re-election, he called a handpicked constituent assembly in 1936. Its constitution allowed immediate re-election, lengthened the presidential term from four to six years, restored the death penalty, reduced the legislature's powers and denied women citizenship and the vote. In 1939 the legislature extended his term to 1949. The Communist Party was outlawed in 1935, the opposition press was cracked down on, and by the end of the decade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> was the only organised party; opposition leaders were imprisoned or exiled. Sources name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tela">Tela</a> massacre of 1937 against the Garífuna people and the killing of more than 70 protesters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-sula">San Pedro Sula</a> on 6 July 1944.</p><p>Honduras declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in 1941. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> pressure Carías stood aside in 1948 and his choice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-galvez">Juan Manuel Gálvez</a>, took office in January 1949 after the Liberals boycotted the election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Carías gave Honduras a long period of relative peace and order. Its finances improved steadily, education improved slightly, the road network grew and the armed forces were modernised, but democratic institutions withered and opposition and labour activity were suppressed. When he sought a return to the presidency in 1954, his candidacy split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>; the Liberal Ramón Villeda Morales won a plurality, and Vice President Julio Lozano Díaz seized power in a coup. Carías died in 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiburcio Carías Andino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A professional army emerged under <b>Tiburcio Carías Andino</b> and, after the constitution of 1957 gave it autonomy, became a political force in its own right.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Tegucigalpa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2163 1638 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tiburcio Carías Andino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2613 1908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2613 1908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
